CARDS = [
# ---------- Node 25 ----------
("node-25", "Node 25 · Permissions", "What is a user, to the kernel?", "An unsigned integer in the process's credentials. Usernames exist only in <code>/etc/passwd</code>, read by ordinary library code in user space to render numbers as names."),
("node-25", "Node 25 · Permissions", "Why does a container writing to a bind mount produce confusing ownership?", "UID 1000 inside and UID 1000 on the host are the same user to the kernel, regardless of what either side's <code>/etc/passwd</code> calls it — unless a user namespace maps them."),
("node-25", "Node 25 · Permissions", "How does the permission check pick which bits apply?", "First match wins: if you are the owner, only the owner bits apply, even if the group bits would grant more. A file you own with mode <code>044</code> is one you cannot read and everyone else can."),
("node-25", "Node 25 · Permissions", "What do r, w and x mean on a <em>directory</em>?", "r lists the names; w creates, deletes and renames entries — which is why deleting a file is governed by the directory's permissions, not the file's; x is search, permitting traversal to something inside. x without r lets you open a file whose exact name you know while preventing listing."),
("node-25", "Node 25 · Permissions", "What are setuid, setgid and the sticky bit?", "setuid runs a program as the file's owner — how <code>passwd</code> edits <code>/etc/shadow</code>, and a permanent source of privilege escalation. setgid does the same for groups and makes directory contents inherit the group. The sticky bit on a directory restricts deletion to each file's owner, which is why <code>/tmp</code> is <code>1777</code>."),
("node-25", "Node 25 · Permissions", "What is umask and why does it surprise people?", "A mask of bits removed from the mode of newly created files — the common <code>022</code> turns a requested 666 into 644. It is per-process state inherited by children, so a daemon's umask can differ from your shell's."),
("node-25", "Node 25 · Permissions", "Why do capabilities exist, and name five.", "Because all-or-nothing root means a server that needs to bind port 80 must start as root, leaving a window in which any bug is total compromise. <code>CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE</code>, <code>CAP_NET_ADMIN</code>, <code>CAP_NET_RAW</code> (why <code>ping</code> often fails in a container), <code>CAP_SYS_PTRACE</code>, and <code>CAP_SYS_ADMIN</code> — a grab-bag including <code>mount</code>, widely called the new root."),
("node-25", "Node 25 · Permissions", "What does SELinux add beyond permission bits, and where do you meet it?", "Mandatory access control: a policy enforced regardless of ordinary permissions and not overridable by the file's owner. You meet it as the <code>:z</code>/<code>:Z</code> volume mount suffixes on Red Hat systems, where the failure without them is a permission denied the bits say should have worked."),
("node-25", "Node 25 · Permissions", "Justify each field of a hardened Kubernetes securityContext.", "<code>runAsNonRoot</code>/<code>runAsUser</code>: not UID 0, so an escape is not host root. <code>allowPrivilegeEscalation: false</code>: sets <code>no_new_privs</code> so setuid binaries cannot elevate. <code>readOnlyRootFilesystem</code>: with an <code>emptyDir</code> where writes are genuinely needed. <code>capabilities: {drop: [ALL]}</code>. <code>seccompProfile: RuntimeDefault</code>. Each is one mechanism from this document, turned off."),
("node-25", "Node 25 · Permissions", "Why do Node apps listen on 3000 or 8080?", "Ports below 1024 historically required root, so the pattern is a high port as a non-root user with nginx, a load balancer or a Service handling 80 and 443. You can also lower <code>net.ipv4.ip_unprivileged_port_start</code>, and Kubernetes generally sidesteps it."),
("node-25", "Node 25 · Permissions", "What are the three correct fixes for volume ownership mismatch?", "Match the UIDs deliberately (build the image with the host user's UID); use a named volume rather than a bind mount so the runtime handles ownership; or in Kubernetes set <code>fsGroup</code>. The genuinely correct answer is user namespaces. <code>chmod 777</code>, running as root, and chowning on startup are all bad."),
("node-25", "Node 25 · Permissions", "Why might <code>fs.writeFile(path, data, {mode: 0o600})</code> not give you what you asked for?", "The mode is subject to umask. Writing a secret or a private key needs an explicit <code>fs.chmod</code> after creation or verification — and <code>ssh</code> silently rejects a group-readable private key, which is a habit worth copying."),
# ---------- Node 26 ----------
("node-26", "Node 26 · Namespaces", "State the namespace mechanism in one sentence.", "Every global resource the kernel names becomes a per-process pointer to a table, so processes sharing a pointer see the same table and processes with different pointers see different ones."),
("node-26", "Node 26 · Namespaces", "Why is a namespace not a restriction?", "It changes what <em>exists</em> as far as that process is concerned. A process in its own PID namespace does not fail to kill PID 4321; there is no PID 4321 in its numbering. Isolation by construction rather than by check — which is why it is cheap and complete for what it covers."),
("node-26", "Node 26 · Namespaces", "Name the seven namespaces and what each virtualises.", "Mount (the mount tree, 2002); PID (the process ID space, 2008); Network (interfaces, routes, firewall rules and the port space, 2009); UTS (hostname, 2006); IPC (System V and POSIX message queues, semaphores, shared memory, 2006); User (UID/GID mapping, completed 2013); Cgroup (position in the cgroup hierarchy, 2016)."),
("node-26", "Node 26 · Namespaces", "Why can two containers both bind port 3000?", "The network namespace has a private port number space. It is the most visible benefit of namespaces and it follows from one sentence."),
("node-26", "Node 26 · Namespaces", "How is a container connected to a network at all?", "A <strong>veth pair</strong> — a virtual cable — with one end inside the namespace and the other attached to a host bridge (<code>docker0</code>), plus iptables NAT rules for published ports."),
("node-26", "Node 26 · Namespaces", "Why is the user namespace the most consequential one?", "It maps UIDs to the parent's, so a process can be root in its namespace with no privilege on the host — the correct solution to volume ownership and the basis of rootless containers. It is also the only namespace an unprivileged user may create, and for that reason a recurring source of kernel privilege-escalation vulnerabilities."),
("node-26", "Node 26 · Namespaces", "Name the three system calls that operate on namespaces and what each is used for.", "<code>clone()</code> with <code>CLONE_NEW*</code> flags creates a process in new namespaces (container creation); <code>unshare()</code> moves the caller into new ones; <code>setns()</code> joins an existing one given a descriptor — which is what <code>docker exec</code> and <code>kubectl exec</code> do."),
("node-26", "Node 26 · Namespaces", "What is <em>not</em> namespaced, and why does each matter?", "The kernel itself (so a kernel bug affects every container and a panic takes them all down — the fundamental difference from a VM); the system clock; most of <code>/proc/sys</code>; and the CPU count and memory totals reported by <code>/proc</code> — which is why <code>os.cpus().length</code>, <code>nproc</code> and <code>lscpu</code> all lie inside a container."),
("node-26", "Node 26 · Namespaces", "Define a Kubernetes pod precisely, and derive four of its behaviours.", "Containers sharing the network and IPC namespaces and usually UTS, with separate mount and PID namespaces. Hence: <code>localhost</code> between them; they cannot both bind 8080; no shared files without a shared volume; and no visibility of each other's processes without <code>shareProcessNamespace</code>."),
("node-26", "Node 26 · Namespaces", "What is the pause container for?", "It holds the pod's network namespace and does nothing but sleep, so the namespace survives individual containers restarting."),
("node-26", "Node 26 · Namespaces", "How does a service mesh sidecar intercept traffic without the application knowing?", "It is in the same network namespace, and iptables rules redirect the application's traffic through it. The application needs no awareness at all."),
("node-26", "Node 26 · Namespaces", "What do Docker's <code>host</code> and <code>none</code> network modes mean mechanically?", "<code>host</code> means <em>not</em> creating a network namespace — the container shares the host's, so there is no NAT, no port mapping, full performance and no isolation. <code>none</code> creates an empty one."),
# ---------- Node 27 ----------
("node-27", "Node 27 · cgroups", "What is a cgroup and how do you configure one?", "A set of processes plus resource controllers, arranged in a hierarchy exposed as a filesystem — so configuring limits is writing text into files under <code>/sys/fs/cgroup</code>. Google, 2006 (as process containers), merged in Linux 2.6.24 in 2008."),
("node-27", "Node 27 · cgroups", "Distinguish <code>cpu.weight</code> from <code>cpu.max</code>.", "<code>cpu.weight</code> is a relative share (1–10000, default 100), CFS's task weight applied to a group — it only matters under contention, and an uncontended group gets everything. <code>cpu.max</code> is a hard quota written as quota and period: at most N µs per period, after which every task in the group is stopped until the next period, even on an idle machine."),
("node-27", "Node 27 · cgroups", "Do the throttling arithmetic for a one-core limit and five busy threads.", "The 100 ms quota is consumed in 20 ms of wall clock, then the container sleeps for 80 ms. Average utilisation reads as 100% of one core, and every request arriving in those 80 ms takes an extra 80 ms. That is latency spikes at apparently fine CPU."),
("node-27", "Node 27 · cgroups", "State the argument for requests without CPU limits, and the counter-argument.", "Requests give a guaranteed share under contention; limits only add throttling and cannot make anything faster, so for latency-sensitive services limits are pure cost. The counter-argument is that without them a misbehaving container degrades its neighbours. Both are defensible; the decision should be made knowingly."),
("node-27", "Node 27 · cgroups", "Name the three memory thresholds and say which Kubernetes exposes.", "<code>memory.max</code> — hard; exceeding it triggers reclaim and then the cgroup OOM killer. <code>memory.high</code> — soft; above it the kernel throttles allocations and reclaims hard, making the group slow rather than dead. <code>memory.min</code>/<code>low</code> — reclaim protection. Kubernetes exposes only the hard one, which is a genuine gap."),
("node-27", "Node 27 · cgroups", "What does <code>pids.max</code> defend against?", "A fork bomb. Without it one container can exhaust the machine's PID space and nothing anywhere can start a process."),
("node-27", "Node 27 · cgroups", "What is PSI and why is it a better signal than utilisation?", "Pressure Stall Information (Linux 4.20, 2018) reports the fraction of time tasks were stalled waiting for CPU, memory or I/O. Utilisation says how busy something is; pressure says how much your work is being delayed, which is what you actually care about. It is what modern autoscaling and <code>oomd</code> use."),
("node-27", "Node 27 · cgroups", "Map Kubernetes resource fields to cgroup files.", "<code>requests.cpu</code> → <code>cpu.weight</code>; <code>limits.cpu</code> → <code>cpu.max</code> (throttling); <code>requests.memory</code> → scheduling and reclaim protection; <code>limits.memory</code> → <code>memory.max</code> (OOM kill)."),
("node-27", "Node 27 · cgroups", "State the asymmetry between CPU and memory limits and explain why it exists.", "Exceeding a CPU limit makes you slow; exceeding a memory limit makes you dead. CPU is compressible — give a process less and it still runs. Memory is not: there is nothing to give back once the bytes are written."),
("node-27", "Node 27 · cgroups", "What are the QoS classes and what do they determine?", "Guaranteed (requests equal limits everywhere), Burstable, and BestEffort (nothing set, <code>oom_score_adj</code> 1000). They determine who the kernel kills first when the <em>node</em> runs out of memory — so setting requests is a survival decision, not just a scheduling hint."),
("node-27", "Node 27 · cgroups", "How does systemd give you container-style limits with no container?", "It puts every service in its own cgroup, so <code>MemoryMax=</code>, <code>CPUQuota=</code> and <code>TasksMax=</code> in a unit file are exactly these mechanisms. <code>systemd-cgtop</code> shows resource use by cgroup."),
# ---------- Node 28 ----------
("node-28", "Node 28 · The container", "Define a container in one sentence.", "An ordinary Linux process — visible in the host's process list with a real PID — started with a particular combination of namespaces, cgroups, a layered root filesystem, a credential mapping, a capability set and a syscall filter. The kernel has no container object."),
("node-28", "Node 28 · The container", "What are the three parts of an OCI image?", "Layers (tar archives of filesystem differences, content-addressed); a config (default command, environment, user, and the ordered layer digests); and a manifest listing both, whose own digest is the image's digest. A manifest list maps architectures to manifests."),
("node-28", "Node 28 · The container", "Why should production reference <code>image@sha256:...</code> rather than a tag?", "Digests are immutable; tags are mutable pointers. It is also why <code>imagePullPolicy: Always</code> exists."),
("node-28", "Node 28 · The container", "Walk the eleven steps of <code>docker run</code>.", "Resolve and pull; assemble the overlay filesystem; create the cgroup and write limits; <code>clone()</code> with namespace flags; wire the veth pair and NAT rules; <code>pivot_root</code> and unmount the old root; mount <code>/proc</code>, <code>/sys</code>, <code>/dev</code>; set the hostname; set UID, drop capabilities, set <code>no_new_privs</code>, install seccomp and the LSM label; set environment, working directory and stdio; <code>execve</code> the entrypoint."),
("node-28", "Node 28 · The container", "Why <code>pivot_root</code> rather than <code>chroot</code>?", "<code>pivot_root</code> unmounts the old root afterwards, so the host filesystem is no longer reachable at all. <code>chroot</code> is escapable."),
("node-28", "Node 28 · The container", "Place runc, containerd, the shim, Docker, Podman and BuildKit.", "runc performs the eleven steps from an OCI bundle. containerd manages images, storage, networking and lifecycle and calls runc; Kubernetes talks to it through the CRI. The shim is one small process per container so containerd can restart without killing containers and so someone collects exit statuses. Docker is a developer-facing layer over containerd. Podman is daemonless and rootless by default. BuildKit is the parallel, cache-mount and secret-mount capable builder."),
("node-28", "Node 28 · The container", "Why did removing Docker support from Kubernetes 1.24 change nothing about your images?", "containerd was underneath all along, and Docker-built images are OCI images."),
("node-28", "Node 28 · The container", "How do you debug a distroless container?", "<code>nsenter -t &lt;host-pid&gt; -n -p -m</code> from the host puts a shell into its namespaces with no tooling in the image; <code>docker inspect --format '{{.State.Pid}}'</code> gets the PID. In Kubernetes, <code>kubectl debug</code> creates an ephemeral container in the pod's namespaces."),
("node-28", "Node 28 · The container", "State a container's security properties honestly.", "Isolation rests entirely on kernel correctness, because there is one kernel and every container calls into it directly, so a reachable privilege-escalation bug is a host compromise. That is a different risk profile from a VM, and it is why untrusted multi-tenant workloads use gVisor, Kata or Firecracker. Mitigations by value: not root, drop all capabilities, seccomp, read-only root, user namespace."),
# ---------- Node 29 ----------
("node-29", "Node 29 · Virtual machines", "How old is virtualisation, and what question was it answering?", "IBM CP-40 and CP-67 from 1967, productised as VM/370 in 1972 and still sold as z/VM. Time-sharing and virtualisation were competing answers to the same question of how to share an expensive machine — one shares an operating system among users, the other gives each user a whole machine."),
("node-29", "Node 29 · Virtual machines", "What is the Popek–Goldberg requirement and why did x86 fail it?", "Every instruction that could reveal or change privileged state must trap when executed unprivileged. Seventeen x86 instructions behaved differently in user mode instead of trapping — <code>popf</code> silently ignoring interrupt-flag changes, for instance — so a guest kernel got wrong answers with no way for the hypervisor to notice."),
("node-29", "Node 29 · Virtual machines", "Name the three historical responses to that failure.", "Binary translation (VMware, 1999 — rewrite the guest's problematic instructions as it executes); paravirtualisation (Xen, 2003 — modify the guest to call the hypervisor explicitly); and hardware virtualisation (Intel VT-x and AMD-V, 2005 — a mode beneath ring 0, which made the cloud economically possible)."),
("node-29", "Node 29 · Virtual machines", "What are second-level address translation and the IOMMU for?", "SLAT (EPT/NPT) lets the MMU walk both the guest's and the hypervisor's page tables in hardware, replacing expensive shadow page tables. The IOMMU translates the addresses a device uses for DMA, which is what makes it safe to hand a physical NIC or GPU directly to a guest."),
("node-29", "Node 29 · Virtual machines", "What is virtio and why does every cloud image boot anywhere?", "Rather than emulating a real device register by register — slow and pointless when the guest knows it is virtualised — virtio defines paravirtual devices with a shared ring-buffer interface. Every Linux guest has the drivers built in."),
("node-29", "Node 29 · Virtual machines", "Compare containers and VMs on six axes.", "Kernel: shared vs its own. Isolation: kernel correctness vs hardware. Start: milliseconds (a <code>clone()</code>) vs seconds (a full boot). Overhead: a process vs a kernel and its memory. Density: hundreds vs tens. Guest OS: must match the host kernel vs anything. Plus: live migration is mature for VMs and essentially absent for containers."),
("node-29", "Node 29 · Virtual machines", "What is Firecracker and why does it exist?", "A minimal KVM-based VMM from AWS (2018, in Rust) emulating five devices, booting a microVM in ~125 ms with ~5 MB overhead. It exists because AWS needed hardware-level isolation between customers' functions with container-like start times. It runs Lambda and Fargate."),
("node-29", "Node 29 · Virtual machines", "Why is the usual production answer both containers and VMs?", "Your Kubernetes nodes are cloud instances: the VM boundary separates tenants, and the container boundary separates your own workloads and gives packaging and density. Presenting it as a choice usually indicates not having run multi-tenant infrastructure."),
("node-29", "Node 29 · Virtual machines", "Explain four Docker Desktop frustrations with one fact.", "Containers run inside a Linux VM because macOS and Windows lack the kernel features. Hence: the memory figure is the VM's allocation; bind mounts cross a filesystem boundary and are slow; <code>--network host</code> means the VM's network, not your Mac's; and <code>host.docker.internal</code> exists to reach the host."),
("node-29", "Node 29 · Virtual machines", "What changed between WSL1 and WSL2, and what does it mean for file performance?", "WSL1 translated Linux syscalls into NT kernel calls — a compatibility layer, fast on Windows files, incomplete, and unable to run Docker. WSL2 runs a real Linux kernel in a lightweight VM, so it is fully compatible, and the filesystem story inverts: files under the Linux root are fast, <code>/mnt/c</code> crosses the boundary. Moving a project inside WSL2 is often a five-to-tenfold build improvement."),
("node-29", "Node 29 · Virtual machines", "Explain a serverless cold start mechanically, and which optimisations follow.", "Booting a microVM plus initialising the runtime plus demand-paging your code. So smaller packages help (less to fault in), provisioned concurrency keeps the VM warm, SnapStart replaces the boot with a restore, and raising memory also raises CPU — which is why a CPU-bound function can be cheaper at a higher memory setting."),
]
